      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_10",
      "quote": "One of the key findings of this meta-analysis is the significant superiority of ESD in reducing recurrence rates of colorectal laterally spreading tumors (LSTs) larger than 20 mm. The overall recurrence rates in the ESD and EMR groups were 0.62% and 12.02%, respectively, which aligns closely with the results of studies such as that by Russo et al. [21]. The higher recurrence rate in the EMR group observed in this meta-analysis is associated with both lesion size and the use of piecemeal resection [14, 22, 23]. This is further supported by the findings of Motomi Terasaki et al., who reported that the local recurrence rate for 40 mm LSTs was significantly higher than that for 20-39 mm lesions ( p < 0.001), and that lesions resected in three or more pieces had a notably higher recurrence rate compared with those resected in two pieces or en bloc ( p = 0.013). Similarly, in the study by Yutaka Saito et al., the recurrence rate was approximately 2% in the ESD group compared with approximately 14% in the EMR group, and recurrence after EMR occurred more frequently in piecemeal resection cases. Therefore, when lesions are large and piecemeal resection is anticipated (especially > 40 mm), ESD may be preferred to reduce the risk of recurrence. In contrast, if EMR can achieve en bloc or limited-piece resection, the recurrence risk appears to be relatively controllable. These results highlight the crucial role of resection completeness and lesion size in recurrence risk. ... A systematic review and meta-analysis by Chandan et al. demonstrated that STSC after EMR for LSTs > 20 mm significantly lowered adenoma recurrence at the first surveillance colonoscopy (pooled rate: 6%; OR 0.27, p < 0.001) [26]. Similarly, a recent meta-analysis of randomized trials by Santo et al. confirmed that thermal ablation (including both STSC and APC) of post-EMR margins reduced recurrence rates (RR = 0.31, p < 0.001) without increasing the risk of adverse events or delayed bleeding [27]. These findings suggest that margin ablation may serve as a safe and effective adjunct to EMR, particularly in settings where ESD is not readily available or feasible.",
      "reason": "The discussion explicitly compares the meta-analysis results with prior studies (e.g., Russo et al., Terasaki, Saito) and other systematic reviews/meta-analyses (Chandan et al., Santo et al.), interpreting findings in the context of lesion size, piecemeal resection, and adjunctive techniques, thereby situating the results within existing evidence."
    }
  ],
